1
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:11,730
I wanted this as MIDI. Every hit, the right drum, the right
loudness.

2
00:00:11,730 --> 00:00:14,510
I thought it would take a weekend.

3
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:20,580
It does work. Open source, runs on your machine, about a
minute a song.

4
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:22,955
It was not a weekend.

5
00:00:22,955 --> 00:00:30,160
I benchmarked it against a $199 product. Twenty-three
hand-annotated recordings.

6
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:35,265
Four rows point our way. Only two survive being checked.

7
00:00:35,265 --> 00:00:40,585
They happen to be the two that matter. Overall, and hi-hat.

8
00:00:40,585 --> 00:00:48,690
An AI agent wrote most of this. Brilliant at code,
pathologically confident about statistics.

9
00:00:48,691 --> 00:00:53,321
It told me we were better at toms. We are not.

10
00:00:53,321 --> 00:00:58,801
Five retractions in one day. Two were compliments to the
competitor.

11
00:00:58,801 --> 00:01:05,766
Being strict paid off. It found a real bug: our tom threshold
was rationing its own toms.

12
00:01:05,766 --> 00:01:10,691
One constant fixed it, and it held on a drummer we never tuned
it on.

13
00:01:10,691 --> 00:01:14,426
Free, open source, runs on your machine.

14
00:01:14,426 --> 00:01:19,541
Measured, not guessed. Eventually.
